  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection with a yellow-zone asthma flare (J06.9)</content>. A cold that has triggered a mild flare: clinic peak flow 300 L/min (68 percent of his best), wheeze without signs of a severe attack, oxygen saturation 95 percent, and a good response to extra as-needed budesonide-formoterol. No sign of pneumonia, so no chest X-ray and no antibiotic, following ACP and CDC advice. He has followed his action plan correctly. [S]<list><item>Continue maintenance inhalations and take extra budesonide-formoterol as needed, no more than 12 inhalations in a day.</item><item>Continue tiotropium.</item><item>Start prednisolone 40 mg daily for 5 days only if the peak flow falls below 264 L/min (60 percent of best), if he needs more than 12 inhalations a day, or if he is no better in 2 to 3 days; he will call the clinic first.</item><item>Red zone (peak flow below 220 L/min, breathless at rest, or not helped by his inhaler): call 911.</item><item>Acetaminophen for aches; honey and warm drinks for the cough; keep away from fires and smoky rooms.</item><item>Asthma review in one week.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma with persistent airflow limitation (J45.40)</content>. Well controlled before this cold; the flare is the first since treatment began. [S]<list><item>Action plan reviewed with him; peak flow twice daily until back to his best.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, asthma and COPD monographs (2026); Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Asthma review in one week; sooner for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 1692-10-22: asthma review two weeks after a cold with a yellow-zone flare. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
